% Which cycle a rejection names, how many rejections there are, and in what
% order.
%
% p/1, q/1, r/1, s/1 and t/1 form one component.  From q/1 there are two
% ways back to p/1, through r/1 and t/1 or through s/1 alone; the shorter is
% named even though the longer is written first.  A second negative edge in
% the same component, from s/1 to t/1, is part of the same mistake and gets
% no error of its own.
%
% lonely/1 negates itself: a cycle of one edge, in a component of its own.
% r/1 reads it, so the analysis finishes lonely/1's component before the
% first one, and it is still reported second, because its negative literal
% is written later.
%
% m/1 and n/1 negate each other.  The fact mentions n/1 first, so the
% analysis meets n/1's negative edge before m/1's, and the error is still at
% m/1's rule, because it is written first.
n(y).
base(x).

p(X) :- base(X), not q(X).
q(X) :- r(X).
q(X) :- s(X).
r(X) :- t(X), lonely(X).
t(X) :- p(X).
s(X) :- p(X), not t(X).

lonely(X) :- base(X), not lonely(X).

m(X) :- base(X), not n(X).
n(X) :- base(X), not m(X).
